# PML-002 — Governance Framework

**Project:** Trading Intelligence Platform (TIP)
**Document ID:** PML-002
**Version:** 1.0.0
**Status:** Approved (Initial Baseline)
**Classification:** Project Governing Standard
**Owner:** TIP Architecture & Engineering Team
**Effective Date:** 2026-08-08

---

# 1. Purpose

The Governance Framework establishes the management, oversight, decision-making authority, quality controls, and accountability required to successfully deliver and maintain the Trading Intelligence Platform (TIP).

Its purpose is to ensure that all project activities are performed in a controlled, traceable, repeatable, and auditable manner while preserving architectural integrity and engineering quality.

---

# 2. Scope

This framework applies to:

* Project planning
* Architecture
* Software development
* Database development
* Artificial Intelligence
* Documentation
* Testing
* Security
* Infrastructure
* Deployment
* Production support
* Maintenance

All contributors shall comply with this governance framework.

---

# 3. Governance Principles

The project shall be governed by the following principles:

* Architecture before implementation.
* Standards before features.
* Quality before delivery.
* Security by design.
* Automation wherever practical.
* Complete lifecycle traceability.
* Controlled change management.
* Continuous improvement.

---

# 4. Governance Structure

The Trading Intelligence Platform shall operate under the following governance structure.

```text
Project Sponsor
        │
        ▼
Product Owner
        │
        ▼
Solution Architect
        │
        ▼
Technical Lead
        │
 ┌──────┼─────────────┬───────────────┐
 ▼      ▼             ▼               ▼
Development   QA & Testing   DevOps   Database & AI
```

Each role has clearly defined responsibilities and decision authority.

---

# 5. Roles and Responsibilities

| Role                | Primary Responsibilities                                       |
| ------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Project Sponsor     | Strategic direction, funding, major approvals                  |
| Product Owner       | Business priorities, backlog ownership, scope decisions        |
| Solution Architect  | Enterprise architecture, technical governance, design approval |
| Technical Lead      | Technical implementation, code quality, mentoring              |
| Software Developers | Feature implementation, unit testing, documentation            |
| QA/Test Engineers   | Verification, validation, test automation                      |
| DevOps Engineer     | CI/CD, deployment, infrastructure automation                   |
| Database Engineer   | Database design, optimisation, migration                       |
| AI Engineer         | AI models, feature engineering, evaluation                     |
| Operations          | Production support, monitoring, incident response              |

In the early stages of the project, one individual may perform multiple roles.

---

# 6. Decision Authority

The following decisions require formal approval.

| Decision                | Approval Authority                     |
| ----------------------- | -------------------------------------- |
| Project Scope           | Product Owner                          |
| Architecture Changes    | Solution Architect                     |
| Coding Standards        | Technical Lead                         |
| Database Schema Changes | Database Engineer & Solution Architect |
| Security Standards      | Security Reviewer / Solution Architect |
| Release Approval        | Product Owner & Technical Lead         |
| Production Deployment   | Release Manager / DevOps               |

Major architectural decisions shall also be documented through an Architecture Decision Record (ADR).

---

# 7. Architecture Governance

All software shall comply with:

* Project Master Library (PML)
* Approved Architecture Decision Records (ADR)
* Volumes 1–4 Architecture Documentation
* Coding Standards
* Security Standards
* Testing Standards

No implementation may knowingly violate these standards without approved exception.

---

# 8. Change Management

All proposed changes shall follow this workflow:

```text
Change Request
      │
      ▼
Impact Assessment
      │
      ▼
Architecture Review
      │
      ▼
Approval / Rejection
      │
      ▼
Implementation
      │
      ▼
Testing
      │
      ▼
Documentation Update
      │
      ▼
Merge & Release
```

Every approved change shall be traceable through Git history.

---

# 9. Quality Gates

The following quality gates shall be satisfied before software progresses to the next stage.

### Gate 1 – Requirements

* Requirements documented.
* Scope approved.

### Gate 2 – Architecture

* Design reviewed.
* Architecture approved.

### Gate 3 – Development

* Coding standards followed.
* Static analysis complete.
* Documentation updated.

### Gate 4 – Testing

* Unit tests passed.
* Integration tests passed.
* Regression tests completed.

### Gate 5 – Release

* Security review complete.
* Performance verified.
* Release approval granted.

A quality gate failure shall prevent progression until resolved.

---

# 10. Engineering Reviews

Formal reviews shall include:

* Architecture Review
* Design Review
* Code Review
* Test Review
* Security Review
* Release Review
* Post-Implementation Review

Review outcomes shall be recorded and retained.

---

# 11. Risk Management

Project risks shall be:

* Identified
* Assessed
* Prioritised
* Assigned
* Mitigated
* Monitored
* Closed

High-impact risks shall be reviewed regularly.

---

# 12. Issue Management

All issues shall be recorded and classified by:

* Severity
* Priority
* Impact
* Owner
* Target Resolution Date
* Current Status

Issues affecting architecture shall be escalated to the Solution Architect.

---

# 13. Communication

The project shall maintain:

* Version-controlled documentation
* Architecture Decision Records
* Sprint planning records
* Release notes
* Change logs
* Technical meeting minutes (where applicable)

Git commit history forms part of the official engineering record.

---

# 14. Compliance

Compliance with this framework is mandatory.

Every contributor shall:

* Follow approved standards.
* Maintain documentation.
* Produce traceable work.
* Participate in reviews.
* Resolve identified defects.
* Protect architectural integrity.

Non-compliance shall be addressed before work is merged into the primary development branch.

---

# 15. Continuous Improvement

The governance framework shall be reviewed periodically.

Improvements may originate from:

* Lessons learned
* Code reviews
* Retrospectives
* Security assessments
* Performance analysis
* Operational experience

Changes shall be managed through the formal change management process.

---

# 16. Governance Statement

The Governance Framework provides the management and engineering controls required to ensure that the Trading Intelligence Platform remains maintainable, secure, scalable, and aligned with its architectural vision throughout its lifecycle.

